-- Q12332: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250-10-45 and SAB No. 108, when an error is immaterial to the period it occurred but correcting it in the current period would be material, the entity records a "little r" revision by correcting Year 2 comparatives without amending prior filings. This prevents material misstatement in either period.
Wrong (A): Charging the full correction to Year 3 would materially distort current-period income, which the iron curtain analysis under SAB 108 prohibits.
Wrong (B): A "Big R" restatement is reserved for errors that were material to the originally issued financial statements. Since the error was immaterial to Year 2, a formal restatement is not required.
Wrong (D): Footnote disclosure alone is insufficient. The financial statements themselves must be corrected to prevent material misstatement in Year 3.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12332;

-- Q12334: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 250-10-45-23, when an error relating to a prior interim period of the same fiscal year is discovered, the previously issued interim data must be restated when presented in later interim or annual reports. This ensures each interim period reflects accurate figures.
Wrong (A): Correcting the entire $80,000 in Q3 would misstate both Q1 (still overstated) and Q3 (artificially reduced), distorting both interim periods.
Wrong (C): This is a factual error, not a change in accounting estimate. Estimate changes involve revised judgments; revenue overstatement is an error requiring correction.
Wrong (D): Deferring to the annual statements is not permitted. ASC 250 requires correction when the error is discovered, not delayed to year-end.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12334;

-- Q12335: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 250-10-45-5, retrospective application restates all periods presented. Switching to FIFO increases inventory by the LIFO reserve, creating a temporary difference since tax remains on LIFO. DTL = LIFO reserve x 25%: $45,000 at 12/31/Y1 and $60,000 at 12/31/Y2. The after-tax cumulative effect adjusts Y1 opening RE by $135,000 (pre-Y1 LIFO reserve x 75%).
Wrong (A): This places the RE adjustment at Y2 opening instead of Y1 opening. Retrospective application requires adjusting the earliest period presented.
Wrong (C): Switching to FIFO increases inventory above tax basis, creating a deferred tax liability, not a deferred tax asset. The book-tax difference is taxable, not deductible.
Wrong (D): The $180,000 figure uses the gross LIFO reserve without netting the 25% tax effect. The correct after-tax RE adjustment is $180,000 x 75% = $135,000.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12335;

-- Q12337: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 250, the Year 1 ending inventory overstatement self-corrects over two years: it understated Year 1 COGS (overstating Year 1 NI) but then overstated Year 2 COGS (understating Year 2 NI) by the same $50,000, netting to zero in cumulative RE. The separate Year 2 understatement of $20,000 overstates Year 2 COGS, leaving RE understated by $20,000.
Wrong (A): This considers only the Year 1 error without recognizing that inventory overstatements are counterbalancing errors that self-correct by the end of Year 2.
Wrong (C): Netting $50,000 minus $20,000 ignores that the Year 1 error fully counterbalances. Only the Year 2 understatement persists in ending RE.
Wrong (D): Adding both errors ($50,000 + $20,000) fails to account for the counterbalancing nature of the Year 1 overstatement, which reversed through Year 2 COGS.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12337;

-- Q12338: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250-10-45-21, a change in reporting entity (e.g., presenting consolidated statements instead of individual-entity statements) requires retrospective adjustment of all prior periods as if the new reporting entity had always existed.
Wrong (A): A change in accounting estimate is applied prospectively under ASC 250-10-45-17, not retrospectively. No prior-period adjustment is made.
Wrong (B): Error corrections use restatement, not the "as-if-always-existed" retrospective treatment. The terminology and mechanics differ from a reporting entity change.
Wrong (D): A change from non-GAAP to GAAP is treated as an error correction (restatement), not as a reporting entity change requiring the as-if-always-existed approach.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12338;

-- Q12339: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250-10-45-17, a change in useful life is a change in estimate applied prospectively. Book value at January 1, Year 6 = $500,000 - (5 x $50,000) = $250,000. With 3 years remaining, Year 6 depreciation = $250,000 / 3 = $83,333.
Wrong (A): $50,000 is the old annual depreciation under the original 10-year life. It ignores the revised estimate entirely.
Wrong (B): $62,500 incorrectly divides the original cost ($500,000) by the new total life (8 years). Prospective treatment uses remaining book value, not original cost.
Wrong (D): $100,000 incorrectly divides the original cost by 5 remaining years under the original estimate, ignoring both the revised life and the proper prospective calculation.$EXPL$
