-- Q12355: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 250, restatement corrects the financial statements but does not undo legal transactions already completed. However, dividends paid from a deficit in restated retained earnings are reclassified as liquidating dividends (return of capital) in the restated statements because they exceeded available earnings.
Wrong (A): Restatement does not require the physical return of dividends. The dividends were legally declared and paid; only their financial statement classification changes.
Wrong (B): While legally valid, the dividends cannot remain classified as ordinary dividends when restated RE shows a deficit. Financial statement reclassification to return of capital is required.
Wrong (D): There is no GAAP concept of adding dividends back to RE for redistribution. The cash has been paid; the restatement affects classification, not the economic transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12355;

-- Q12358: Accounting Changes and Error Corrections [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 250-10-20, the cumulative effect of a change in accounting principle is the difference between the carrying amounts of assets and liabilities (and thus retained earnings) at the beginning of the earliest period presented under the old versus new principle. This adjusts opening retained earnings.
Wrong (B): The total effect on a single year''s net income is not the cumulative effect. The cumulative effect encompasses all periods prior to the earliest period presented.
Wrong (C): The difference in total assets at the end of the current year measures a point-in-time comparison, not the cumulative effect used for the opening RE adjustment.
Wrong (D): The tax impact is one component of the cumulative effect calculation but does not define it. The cumulative effect is the full net-of-tax difference in carrying amounts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12358;

-- Q12360: Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10, effective ownership through a multi-tier structure is computed by multiplying successive ownership percentages. Waverly''s indirect interest in Finch = 80% x 60% = 48%.
Wrong (A): 60% is Dalton''s direct ownership of Finch, ignoring the intermediate ownership link. Waverly does not own 100% of Dalton, so it cannot claim Dalton''s full 60%.
Wrong (B): 80% is Waverly''s ownership of Dalton, not its interest in Finch. The ownership chain must be traced through the subsidiary to compute the effective indirect interest.
